::STAT_U03_L01_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which statement best describes correlation?

{
=A. A relationship between two numerical variables#Correct. Correlation describes how two numerical variables are related.
~B. Proof that one variable causes another#Correlation does not prove causation.
~C. A table with one categorical variable#That describes univariate categorical data, not correlation.
~D. A random assignment method#Random assignment is a study design feature, not correlation.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A scatterplot shows that higher outdoor temperature is linked with more water bottles sold. What conclusion is safest?

{
~A. Temperature proves every person buys water#This is too absolute and claims more than the data show.
=B. Temperature is associated with water bottle sales#Correct. This accurately describes the relationship shown.
~C. Water bottle sales cause hot weather#This reverses the likely relationship and is not supported.
~D. There is no association#The described pattern shows an association.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
As hours of video gaming increase, homework completion tends to decrease. What direction is the association?

{
~A. Positive#Positive means both variables tend to increase together.
~B. No clear association#The statement describes a clear pattern.
=C. Negative#Correct. One variable increases while the other tends to decrease.
~D. Causal by definition#Direction of association does not prove causation.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which question helps identify a possible confounding variable?

{
~A. Are the variables spelled correctly?#Spelling does not evaluate the statistical relationship.
~B. Is the graph colorful?#Color does not determine whether a relationship is causal.
~C. Did the data use decimals?#Decimals do not identify another explanatory variable.
=D. Could another factor explain both variables?#Correct. A confounding variable may explain the association.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A survey shows students with part-time jobs report better time management. What should you avoid claiming?

{
=A. Part-time jobs caused better time management#Correct. A survey association does not prove the job caused the skill.
~B. The variables may be associated#This is a careful claim that may be supported.
~C. There may be another explanation#This is a responsible caution.
~D. The result is about the surveyed students#This appropriately limits the conclusion.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which phrase is usually safest when a study only shows a pattern?

{
~A. is guaranteed by#Guarantee is too strong for an observed pattern.
=B. is associated with#Correct. This describes a pattern without overclaiming causation.
~C. is always caused by#Always caused by is an unsupported causal claim.
~D. has no connection to#A pattern suggests some connection may exist.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Two categorical variables are compared in a table. Can association be discussed?

{
~A. No, association only applies to equations#Association can be discussed for categorical data too.
~B. No, tables cannot show relationships#Two-way tables can show relationships between categories.
=C. Yes, categorical data can show association#Correct. Categorical variables can be associated in a two-way table.
~D. Yes, and it always proves causation#Association in categorical data still does not prove causation.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A researcher wants to support a causal claim. Which design feature is most helpful?

{
~A. A bigger font on the graph#Visual style does not support causation.
~B. A single survey question#A single survey question is usually weak for causation.
~C. Ignoring possible confounders#Ignoring confounders makes causal claims weaker.
=D. Random assignment in an experiment#Correct. Random assignment helps separate treatment effects from other factors.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which statement is an example of a causal claim?

{
=A. Extra tutoring caused the score increase#Correct. The word caused makes this a causal claim.
~B. Extra tutoring is related to scores#This is association language.
~C. Extra tutoring and scores were recorded#This only describes data collection.
~D. Extra tutoring varied among students#This describes variation, not causation.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A pattern slopes upward from left to right. What association is shown?

{
~A. Negative association#Negative association slopes downward.
=B. Positive association#Correct. Upward patterns show positive association.
~C. No association#A clear upward pattern shows association.
~D. Impossible association#An upward pattern is a common association.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Why is "correlation proves causation" incorrect?

{
~A. Correlation never uses two variables#Correlation does use two numerical variables.
~B. Causation is always impossible#Causation can be supported by stronger study designs.
=C. Another variable may explain the relationship#Correct. Confounding variables can create or affect an observed correlation.
~D. Correlation only applies to colors#Correlation applies to numerical variables, not colors specifically.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which conclusion is most careful?

{
~A. The app caused every student to improve#This is too strong and causal.
~B. The app guarantees higher grades#Guarantee is too strong for statistical evidence.
~C. The app has no relationship to grades#This ignores a possible pattern if one was observed.
=D. App use is associated with higher grades in the data#Correct. This is careful and contextual.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
What must be identified first when describing association?

{
=A. The two variables being compared#Correct. Association describes a relationship between variables.
~B. The color of the chart#Chart color does not define the relationship.
~C. The name of the calculator#Technology is not needed to identify variables.
~D. The final grade only#One variable alone is not enough for association.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A data set shows no visible pattern between shoe size and quiz score. What is the best conclusion?

{
~A. Shoe size causes quiz score#No pattern and no causal evidence support this.
=B. No clear association is shown#Correct. The data do not show a visible relationship.
~C. Quiz score causes shoe size#This is unsupported and unreasonable.
~D. The association is strongly positive#A strong positive association would show an upward pattern.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which phrase signals an unsupported overclaim when only association is shown?

{
~A. tends to be related to#This is careful association language.
~B. appears connected with#This is careful because it avoids proving causation.
=C. definitely causes#Correct. Definitely causes is too strong unless causal evidence exists.
~D. is associated with#This is appropriate for observed relationships.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A graph shows study time and test score rising together. What can be said?

{
~A. Study time has no connection to score#The rising pattern suggests a relationship.
~B. Score definitely causes study time#This reverses causation and is unsupported.
~C. Study time guarantees a perfect score#A trend does not guarantee an individual result.
=D. Study time and score have a positive association#Correct. Both variables tend to increase together.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which situation is most likely bivariate?

{
=A. Recording each student's height and backpack weight#Correct. Two variables are measured for each student.
~B. Listing only favorite colors#That is one categorical variable.
~C. Counting only the number of students present#That is one count, not paired variables.
~D. Naming the school mascot#That is not a bivariate data set.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
A health article says sleeping more is linked with lower stress. What should a careful reader ask?

{
~A. Is the title written in bold?#Bold text does not evaluate the claim.
=B. Does the evidence support causation or only association?#Correct. This checks whether the conclusion matches the study evidence.
~C. Are the numbers written in alphabetical order?#Alphabetical order is not relevant to causation.
~D. Did every person have the same name?#Names do not evaluate the statistical relationship.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
Which conclusion goes beyond the evidence if data only show an association?

{
~A. The variables may be related#This stays within association language.
~B. The pattern is positive#This describes direction if supported by the data.
=C. One variable caused the other#Correct. Causation requires stronger evidence than association alone.
~D. The relationship should be interpreted in context#This is good statistical practice.
}


::STAT_U03_L01_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L01_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
What is a confounding variable?

{
~A. The answer choice marked correct#That is test structure, not statistics.
~B. A variable that is always ignored safely#Ignoring it can lead to wrong conclusions.
~C. A variable that proves there is no association#A confounder does not automatically remove association.
=D. Another factor that may explain an observed relationship#Correct. Confounders can affect how association is interpreted.
}


